  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe shortwave passage in underway, as of this afternoon,\nshifting the lake-enhanced showers southwest. Diminishing\ncoverage and intensity of showers is expected as the upstream \nenvironment loses its Huron- Erie fetch and become more \nnortherly. Nonetheless, a few showers remain possible into late\nafternoon/evening time off of Erie, but impacts will be \nlimited. \n\nThe only remaining observations below freezing are for eastern \nTucker County, where perhaps a bit of snow and/or freezing \ndrizzle is possible, but with daytime surface temperatures above\nfreezing, impacts are expected to be limited. Higher elevation \nlight accumulations remain possible tonight, but even then, \nREFS puts the probability of any additional accumulation at \nonly 30%. After precipitation shuts off overnight, the next \nchance of any precipitation doesn't come again until next \nSunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs aforementioned, the outlook for the week is dry and warm as \na ridge establishes and persists over the area. Overcast skies \nwill begin to erode Monday, leaving mostly sunny skies for most\nof the week. There a 75% chance that the Pittsburgh metro \nbreaks 60F for highs Thursday through Sunday (10-15 degrees \nabove average).\n\nSome small uncertainties creep into the forecast Thursday\nthrough Saturday, mostly tied to whether the ridge amplifies\nmore (warmer than the forecast by a few degrees), vs. if it \nremains flattened (cooler than the forecast by a few degrees, \nbut still above average). But the largest uncertainties arrive\nSunday into Monday with uncertainty in the timing or occurrence\nof a ridging breakdown.",
